WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.096
This is the flow I built

00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:06.192
to take a GTA 6 channel to millions of views.

00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:10.388
One prompt. No team. No editor.

00:00:11.388 --> 00:00:16.956
And research that used to eat a full day… now takes one sentence.

00:00:17.456 --> 00:00:21.992
Sound like a thumbnail promise? Good. Keep that doubt.

00:00:22.492 --> 00:00:26.128
Because everything you're about to see

00:00:26.128 --> 00:00:29.764
is the actual machine — my screen, my folders, my queue.

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:36.788
And by the end… the thing that decides whether millions ever happen is not the tools.

00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:39.856
It's one thing you can't automate.

00:00:40.356 --> 00:00:43.212
Let me show you what this looked like before.

00:00:43.712 --> 00:00:50.576
One niche. GTA 6. Trending, ruthless, moving every hour.

00:00:51.076 --> 00:00:56.884
Research: a full day. Trends. Competitors. Outliers. Keywords.

00:00:57.384 --> 00:01:03.288
Then the script. Then the storyboard. Then the artwork. Then the upload.

00:01:03.788 --> 00:01:08.660
One video. One day. And then… do it again tomorrow.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.448
That's the wall. Not talent. Time.

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:18.584
So the machine's first job was simple: steal the day back.

00:01:19.084 --> 00:01:25.852
One prompt. Scan the niche. Seven days of data — views, velocity, channel size.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:31.380
And it finds what a human scrolls past:

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.408
a one-hundred-and-forty-four-subscriber channel… with twelve thousand views.

00:01:36.908 --> 00:01:38.708
Ninety times its size.

00:01:39.708 --> 00:01:43.452
That's the real secret of the millions you're chasing.

00:01:43.952 --> 00:01:47.072
The wave does not check your subscriber count.

00:01:47.572 --> 00:01:52.708
But data alone sells nothing. Something had to make it a story.

00:01:53.208 --> 00:01:58.432
Same prompt. One folder. Inside: the hook. The beats.

00:01:58.432 --> 00:02:03.656
A nine-panel storyboard. Camera directions. Titles.

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:08.880
Two-word thumbnail text. Tags. Chapters — checked against YouTube's rules before anything ships.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.796
That used to be my entire day. Now it's the first paragraph of it.

00:02:14.796 --> 00:02:18.084
But here's where most automation videos lie to you…

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:24.272
…the part where they say the machine makes the video. It doesn't.

00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:27.964
It drafts. It tries. It gets it wrong.

00:02:28.464 --> 00:02:33.372
Google Flow gives fifty credits a day. Five clips.

00:02:33.372 --> 00:02:38.280
One character reference — same face, same palette, every scene.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.636
And a human still says no to the bad ones.

00:02:42.136 --> 00:02:47.104
The machine buys you time. Taste is still the toll.

00:02:48.104 --> 00:02:51.800
And that toll gets paid in the strangest place…

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:58.492
…the queue. A second agent assembles — narration, subtitles, thumbnails.

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:04.464
Drive fills up. One folder per video. Five gigabytes of proof.

00:03:04.964 --> 00:03:10.396
Then the same prompt writes to the channel: upload. Private.

00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:15.828
Scheduled for twenty hundred UTC — America, Britain, Germany, Australia — one hour.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:21.260
Playlist. Comment. Analytics. The loop closes.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.764
Which brings us to the part nobody shows you.

00:03:26.264 --> 00:03:31.496
The machine does not make millions. No system does. It makes time.

00:03:31.996 --> 00:03:37.300
Ten thousand units a day. Fifty credits. Six uploads.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.984
And the creators who win aren't the ones who automate the most…

00:03:42.984 --> 00:03:48.168
they're the ones who automate the boring part, and spend the saved hours being different.

00:03:48.668 --> 00:03:53.084
The wave doesn't check your subscriber count. It checks your patience.

00:03:54.084 --> 00:04:01.020
So — this is the machine. One prompt. One loop. One person.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.320
Comment 'PROMPT' — and if enough of you want it,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.120
part two publishes the raw files. Every skill. Every script. The whole desk.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:16.420
Look beyond what seems obvious. Millions was never the goal.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:17.856
The patience was.

